Proof. We first show containment in NP. Note that a total elimination sequence is a permutation
of I and therefore can be encoded in polynomial space. Moreover, given such a sequence, we can
compute its cost in polynomial time by computing the vertex eliminations one after another.
    To show hardness, we reduce from Vertex Cover. It is well known that Vertex Cover
is NP-hard and cannot be solved in 2o(n+m) time unless the ETH fails [13, 14]. We will provide
a linear reduction from Vertex Cover to Structural Optimal Jacobian Accumulation
thereby proving the theorem. To this end, let (G = (V, E), k) be an input instance of Vertex
Cover. Let n = |V | and m = |E|. We create an equivalent instance (D = (S ⊎ I, A), k ′ ) of
Structural Optimal Jacobian Accumulation as follows. For each vertex v ∈ V , we create
five vertices v1 , v2 , v3 , v4 , v5 . The vertices v1 , v4 and v5 are contained in S for all v ∈ V . Vertices v2
and v3 are contained in I. Next, we add the set {(v1 , v2 ), (v2 , v3 ), (v2 , v4 ), (v2 , v5 ), (v3 , v4 ), (v3 , v5 )} of
arcs to A for each v ∈ V . Finally, for each edge {u, v} ∈ E, we add the arcs (u1 , v3 ), (u2 , v3 ), (v1 , u3 )
and (v2 , u3 ) to A. Notice that every arc goes from a lower-indexed vertex to a higher-indexed vertex.
Hence, the constructed digraph is a DAG. To finish the construction, we set k ′ = 6m + 4n + k. An
illustration of the construction is depicted in Figure 1.
    Since the reduction can clearly be computed in polynomial time, it only remains to show
correctness. We proceed to show that (G, k) is a yes-instance of Vertex Cover if and only if the
constructed instance (D, k ′ ) is a yes-instance of Structural Optimal Jacobian Accumulation.
    First, assume that G contains a vertex cover C of size at most k. We show that eliminating all
vertices v2 with v ∈ C first, then u3 for all u ∈ V \ C, followed by u2 for all u ∈ V \ C, and finally all
vertices v3 for each v ∈ C results in a total cost of k ′ . To see this, note that the cost of eliminating v2
for any vertex v ∈ C is deg(v) + 3 as v2 has a single in-neighbor v1 , three out-neighbors v3 , v4 , and v5 ,
and deg(v) out-neighbors u3 (one for each u ∈ N (v)). The cost of eliminating u3 for any u ∈                           /C
afterwards is 2 deg(u) + 2 as by construction u3 has in-neighbors {w1 | w ∈ N (u)} ∪ {u2 }, two
out-neighbors u4 , u5 and no in-neighbors in {w2 | w ∈ N (u)}, as each w ∈ N (u) is by definition in C
and hence the corresponding w2 has been eliminated before. The cost of eliminating u2 for u ∈ V \ C
is afterwards deg(u) + 2 as u2 has the single in-neighbor u1 , two out-neighbors u4 and u5 and deg(u)
out-neighbors {v3 | v ∈ C} (note that u cannot have neighbors in V \ C since C is a vertex cover
and u ∈ / C). Finally, the cost of eliminating v3 for any v ∈ C is 2 deg(v) + 2 since v3 has deg(v) + 1
in-neighbors {w1 | w ∈ N [v]} and two out-neighbors v4 and v5 . Summing these costs over all vertices



