631:\section{Algorithms}
632:\label{sec:algorithm}
634:In this section, we give two simple algorithms that show that \MinCost{} and \MinRep{} can be solved in~$O^*(2^n)$ time.
637:\begin{proposition}
638:	\MinRep{} can be solved in~$O(2^nn^3)$ time and with polynomial space.
643:	Since there are~$2^n$ possible subsets and each of the at most~$n$ eliminations for each subset can be computed in~$O(n^2)$ time, all subsets can be tested in~$O(2^nn^3)$ time.
646:We continue with \MinCost, where we use an algorithmic framework due to Bodlaender et al.~\cite{bodlaender2012note}.
648:\begin{proposition}
649:	\MinCost{} can be solved in~$O(2^nn^4)$ time. It can also be solved in~$O(4^nn^3)$ time using polynomial space.
653:	As shown by Bodlaender et al.~\cite{bodlaender2012note}, any vertex ordering problem can be solved in~$O(2^nn^{c+1})$ time and in~$O(4^nn^c)$ time using polynomial space if it can be reformulated as~$\min_{\pi} \sum_{v \in V} f(D,\pi_{<v},v)$, where~$\pi$ is a permutation of the vertices, $\pi_{<v}$ is the set of all vertices that appear before~$v$ in~$\pi$, and~$f$ can be computed in~$O(n^c)$ time.      
663:\section{Conclusion}
665:We have resolved a pair of longstanding open questions by showing that \MinCost{} and \MinRep{} are both \NP-complete.
668:both problems under the more expressive \emph{edge elimination} operation~\cite{naumann2002elimination}.
669:There are also promising opportunities to develop approximation algorithms and/or establish lower bounds.
