The second hard call remains in progress. I’m keeping the default 900-second deadline because shortening it would weaken the evidence and could turn latency into a false hardness claim.
diff --git a/results/2506.17521/gen_2506_17521.py b/results/2506.17521/gen_2506_17521.py
index 71e2e0d12622b37226589476ebbee9950a760116..d322ccf11c51d53ec995c9ca3254feff4d2ba7f1
--- a/results/2506.17521/gen_2506_17521.py
+++ b/results/2506.17521/gen_2506_17521.py
@@ -1,15 +1,17 @@
-"""Verified generator for Structural Optimal Jacobian Accumulation.
+"""Verified problem generator for arXiv:2506.17521.
 
-The family uses the exact Vertex Cover gadget in Section 3, Theorem 1 of
-arXiv:2506.17521.  A cover is sampled by first sampling its independent-set
-complement, a cubic source graph is built around it, and the theorem's
-four-phase total vertex-elimination sequence is carried into the resulting
-computational DAG.  Verification compiles any candidate cover and replays the
-eliminations with exact integer graph operations.
+The generated problem is Structural Optimal Jacobian Accumulation in the
+paper's vertex-elimination model. Section 3, Theorem 1 maps a Vertex Cover
+instance to a computational DAG and gives a four-phase elimination sequence
+for every cover. We generate the complement of a cover first, as a hidden
+independent set, then carry that witness through the theorem's construction.
 
-There are no import-time side effects and the implementation is standard-
-library-only.  ``gvlib`` is made available when the repository helper package
-is present, but this finite graph family does not require it.
+This is an honest Track B family. A public finite-field tag gives an O(n)
+decoder for the planted independent set, while a solver that misses the tag
+faces a regular planted Independent Set instance and, beyond it, an ordering
+problem on twice as many internal DAG vertices. The checker never trusts the
